1
00:00:15,223 --> 00:00:17,678
Once upon a time,

2
00:00:17,702 --> 00:00:22,000
there was a railway company that kept using Windows XP.

3
00:00:22,816 --> 00:00:27,635
And it became a plaything for black-hat hackers.

4
00:00:28,694 --> 00:00:32,028
Yes, that was our company five years ago.

5
00:00:32,275 --> 00:00:34,274
And now...

6
00:00:34,880 --> 00:00:36,608
CIO!

7
00:00:37,164 --> 00:00:40,144
Windows 7 support ends in one month!

8
00:00:40,250 --> 00:00:42,210
Approve the budget immediately!

9
00:00:42,770 --> 00:00:44,797
...But we don't have any money.

10
00:00:45,803 --> 00:00:47,163
...

11
00:00:47,529 --> 00:00:50,000
I'm getting deja vu here.

12
00:00:50,916 --> 00:00:53,000
We do have money, actually.

13
00:00:54,023 --> 00:00:57,730
You know our bestselling ponzu sauce from the side business?

14
00:00:57,963 --> 00:01:01,000
The profits from that can cover the monthly license fees.

15
00:01:01,518 --> 00:01:05,000
Even so, there's this thing called departmental budgets...

16
00:01:05,485 --> 00:01:10,673
Then if the System Division sells it and books the revenue,
we can secure the budget, right?

17
00:01:10,807 --> 00:01:12,806
Tarumi-senpai is already setting it up.

18
00:01:13,073 --> 00:01:14,306
You can't do that!

19
00:01:14,331 --> 00:01:15,846
Sales will get mad at us!

20
00:01:17,001 --> 00:01:19,369
...Fine, I get it.

21
00:01:19,393 --> 00:01:22,736
I'll approve the budget, so stop doing weird things.

22
00:01:22,803 --> 00:01:25,000
And that's how internal politics work.

23
00:01:25,721 --> 00:01:30,000
At long last, the OS upgrade project began.

24
00:01:31,419 --> 00:01:34,161
Well, we had already resolved

25
00:01:34,185 --> 00:01:36,461
a lot of bugs in our virtual test environment, so--

26
00:01:36,486 --> 00:01:39,399
All that's left is to deploy to the actual machines.

27
00:01:41,125 --> 00:01:42,528
!?

28
00:01:43,591 --> 00:01:46,208
It won't boot no matter what we do?

29
00:01:46,895 --> 00:01:51,554
The cause was a CPU too old to support the NX bit.

30
00:01:53,350 --> 00:01:55,983
Two days until support ends.

31
00:01:56,856 --> 00:02:01,466
Unexpected issues kept popping up, and we fought to resolve them.

32
00:02:02,250 --> 00:02:05,000
A 16-bit application won't run, they say.

33
00:02:05,156 --> 00:02:10,686
Ugh... We'll have to use the 32-bit version of Windows 10 as an exception.

34
00:02:11,845 --> 00:02:13,225
Six hours left.

35
00:02:13,534 --> 00:02:16,110
The web app's layout is broken, they say.

36
00:02:16,135 --> 00:02:21,055
Shall we add an "X-UA-Compatible" HTTP header?

37
00:02:21,814 --> 00:02:23,835
30 seconds left.

38
00:02:24,309 --> 00:02:26,903
Akane! You look terrible!

39
00:02:26,927 --> 00:02:28,935
You should go home already.

40
00:02:30,068 --> 00:02:35,172
I'm sorry... I don't think I can be of help anymore.

41
00:02:35,386 --> 00:02:39,648
The last bug...

42
00:02:39,672 --> 00:02:43,849
...resolved.

43
00:02:44,145 --> 00:02:46,819
After 55 hours of continuous work,

44
00:02:46,843 --> 00:02:49,554
the task was finally complete.
